From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brad Barnett <127794626+bdbarnett@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 00:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] py/runtime_utils: An error printing a callback's exception
 must not escape.

A scheduled callback that overflows the stack can stop every scheduled
callback for the rest of the run. mp_call_function_1_protected() catches the
overflow and prints it, but printing checks the stack as well: the check in
mp_obj_print_helper() raises again, this time with no handler left in the
protected call. The exception jumps out through mp_sched_run_pending(), past
its mp_sched_unlock(), and lands in whatever Python frame was running.
sched_state stays at MP_SCHED_LOCKED until the next soft reset, and the
foreground gets a RuntimeError it did nothing to cause.

Seen on RP2040, ESP32-S3, ESP32-P4 and the unix port by scheduling a no-op with
the foreground within a few hundred bytes of the stack limit. The console shows
"RuntimeError: " with no message, then no machine.Timer callback runs again
and micropython.schedule() fills up and raises "schedule queue full".

Catch whatever the print raises, so a protected call does what its name says,
and say so on the console. The callback's error is still reported as far as
there's stack to print it, and the foreground carries on.

diff --git a/py/runtime_utils.c b/py/runtime_utils.c
index 3b0ae279a..f6b55986b 100644
--- a/py/runtime_utils.c
+++ b/py/runtime_utils.c
@@ -27,6 +27,21 @@
 
 #include "py/runtime.h"
 
+// Printing an exception can raise too: mp_obj_print_helper() checks the stack,
+// and after a stack overflow this runs about as deep as the overflow did.  An
+// exception that got out would skip what the caller does after a protected call,
+// such as the scheduler unlocking itself.
+static void print_exception_protected(mp_obj_t exc) {
+    nlr_buf_t nlr;
+    if (nlr_push(&nlr) == 0) {
+        mp_obj_print_exception(&mp_plat_print, exc);
+        nlr_pop();
+    } else {
+        // This can end a line the print started, so it reads either way.
+        mp_print_str(&mp_plat_print, "could not print exception\n");
+    }
+}
+
 mp_obj_t mp_call_function_1_protected(mp_obj_t fun, mp_obj_t arg) {
     nlr_buf_t nlr;
     if (nlr_push(&nlr) == 0) {
@@ -34,7 +49,7 @@ mp_obj_t mp_call_function_1_protected(mp_obj_t fun, mp_obj_t arg) {
         nlr_pop();
         return ret;
     } else {
-        mp_obj_print_exception(&mp_plat_print, MP_OBJ_FROM_PTR(nlr.ret_val));
+        print_exception_protected(MP_OBJ_FROM_PTR(nlr.ret_val));
         return MP_OBJ_NULL;
     }
 }
@@ -46,7 +61,7 @@ mp_obj_t mp_call_function_2_protected(mp_obj_t fun, mp_obj_t arg1, mp_obj_t arg2
         nlr_pop();
         return ret;
     } else {
-        mp_obj_print_exception(&mp_plat_print, MP_OBJ_FROM_PTR(nlr.ret_val));
+        print_exception_protected(MP_OBJ_FROM_PTR(nlr.ret_val));
         return MP_OBJ_NULL;
     }
 }
